<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch6-section-lagrangian" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Lagrangian perspective</title>

  <p>
    As introduced in <xref ref="ch1-section-calculus-subsection-total-differentiation" text="custom">Chapter 1</xref>,
    a property <m>f</m> is <term>conserved</term> for an air parcel if
  </p>

  <p>
    <md>
        \dfrac{Df}{Dt} = 0
    </md>
    
  </p>

  <p>
    If an air parcel does not exchange mass with its environment<mdash />a common assumption<mdash />its mass <m>m</m> is conserved and
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-mass-conservation-simple" number="yes">
      \frac{Dm}{Dt} = 0
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    We introduced <term>mass conservation</term> for an air parcel in <xref ref="ch3-section-newtons-laws" text="custom">Chapter 3</xref>
    when simplifying Newton's <m>2^{\text{nd}}</m> law.
    Now we will build on it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Since <m>m = \rho V</m>,
    where <m>\rho</m> is the air parcel's density and <m>V</m> is its volume,
    <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-simple" text="type-global" /> becomes
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-mass-conservation-density" number="yes">
      \frac{D(\rho V)}{Dt} = 0
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    Applying the product rule to <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-density" text="type-global" /> gives
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-mass-conservation-density-expanded" number="yes">
      V \frac{D \rho}{Dt} + \rho \frac{DV}{Dt} = 0
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    which shows that,
    although the air parcel's mass can be unchanging with time,
    its density and volume can change with time in proportion to each other to maintain constant mass.
  </p>

  <p>
    Dividing <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-density-expanded" text="type-global" /> by <m>\rho V</m> produces
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-mass-conservation-density-expanded-divided" number="yes">
      \frac{1}{\rho} \frac{D \rho}{Dt} + \frac{1}{V} \frac{DV}{Dt} = 0
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    Since the air parcel we have been discussing since <xref ref="chapter-3" text="type-global" />
    is a rectangular prism with length <m>\delta x</m>, width <m>\delta y</m> and height <m>\delta z</m>,
    its volume is <m>V = \delta x \, \delta y \, \delta z</m>.
    We can expand the second term in <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-density-expanded-divided" text="type-global" />
    by inserting this definition and applying the product rule.
  </p>

  <p>
    <md>
        <mrow>
            \frac{1}{V} \frac{DV}{Dt} \amp = \frac{1}{\delta x \, \delta y \, \delta z} \frac{D(\delta x \, \delta y \, \delta z)}{Dt}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
            \amp = \frac{1}{\delta x \, \delta y \, \delta z}
            \left[
                \delta y \, \delta z \frac{D \delta x}{Dt}
                + \delta x \, \delta z \frac{D \delta y}{Dt}
                + \delta x \, \delta y \frac{D \delta z}{Dt}
            \right]
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
            \amp = \frac{1}{\delta x} \frac{D \delta x}{Dt} + \frac{1}{\delta y} \frac{D \delta y}{Dt} + \frac{1}{\delta z} \frac{D \delta z}{Dt}
        </mrow>
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    Since <m>\dfrac{D \delta x}{Dt} = \delta u</m>,
    <m>\dfrac{D \delta y}{Dt} = \delta v</m>,
    and <m>\dfrac{D \delta z}{Dt} = \delta w</m>
    give the time rates at which the dimensions of the air parcel are lengthening or shortening,
  </p>

  <p>
    <md>
        \frac{1}{V} \frac{DV}{Dt}
        =
        \frac{\delta u}{\delta x}
        + \frac{\delta v}{\delta y}
        + \frac{\delta w}{\delta z}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    As <m>\delta x \to 0</m>, <m>\delta y \to 0</m>, and <m>\delta z \to 0</m>
    (which is an acceptable assumption for an immeasurably small air parcel),
  </p>

  <p>
    <md>
        \frac{1}{V} \frac{DV}{Dt}
        = \frac{\partial u}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial v}{\partial y} + \frac{\partial w}{\partial z}
        = \nabla \cdot \vec{u}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-density-expanded-divided" text="type-global" /> becomes
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-mass-conservation-lagrangian-1" number="yes">
      \frac{1}{\rho} \frac{D \rho}{Dt} + \nabla \cdot \vec{u} = 0
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    which often is multiplied by <m>\rho</m> to produce the form
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-mass-conservation-lagrangian-2" number="yes">
      \frac{D \rho}{Dt} + \rho \nabla \cdot \vec{u} = 0
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    Either of <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-lagrangian-1" text="type-global" />
    and <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-lagrangian-2" text="type-global" />
    is called the <term>velocity divergence form of mass conservation</term>
    since <m>\nabla \cdot \vec{u}</m> is the <term>velocity divergence</term>,
    i.e., the divergence of the three-dimensional wind.
    Velocity divergence quantifies the rate at which outflow of wind causes an air parcel to expand.
  </p>

  <p>
    Keep in mind that mass conservation does not hold if an air parcel exchanges mass with its environment.
    If, for example, water is added to an air parcel through evaporation
    or removed from it as precipitation particles form within the air parcel and fall out of it,
    <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-lagrangian-1" text="type-global" />
    and <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-lagrangian-2" text="type-global" />
    must be modified to include source or sink terms.
    In cases like these, we can use <m>\sigma</m>
    [<m>\text{kg} \, \text{m}^{-3} \, \text{s}^{-1}</m>]
    to represent a combination of any sources and sinks within the air parcel that cause its mass (and therefore its density) to change.
    For example, <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-lagrangian-2" text="type-global" /> would become:
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-mass-conservation-lagrangian-2-source" number="yes">
      \frac{D \rho}{Dt} + \rho \nabla \cdot \vec{u} = \sigma
    </md>
  </p>

</section>